{"files": [{"id": 47255869, "name": "ie3c03024_si_001.pdf", "size": 622769, "is_link_only": false, "download_url": "https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/47255869", "supplied_md5": "21e1f74ecef541798a50a3bce43f8f92", "computed_md5": "21e1f74ecef541798a50a3bce43f8f92", "mimetype": "application/pdf"}], "folder_structure": {}, "authors": [{"id": 18885865, "full_name": "Hyeon-won Jeong", "first_name": "Hyeon-won", "last_name": "Jeong", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": ""}, {"id": 18885868, "full_name": "Vinh Khanh Nguyen", "first_name": "Vinh Khanh", "last_name": "Nguyen", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": ""}, {"id": 180520, "full_name": "Shu Wang", "first_name": "Shu", "last_name": "Wang", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": ""}, {"id": 18885871, "full_name": "Ricardo Gutfraind", "first_name": "Ricardo", "last_name": "Gutfraind", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": ""}, {"id": 1808098, "full_name": "Ruichang Xiong", "first_name": "Ruichang", "last_name": "Xiong", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": ""}, {"id": 18885874, "full_name": "Jerry Wareck", "first_name": "Jerry", "last_name": "Wareck", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": ""}, {"id": 18885877, "full_name": "Samantha Neilsen", "first_name": "Samantha", "last_name": "Neilsen", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": ""}, {"id": 18885880, "full_name": "W. Jaewoo Shim", "first_name": "W. Jaewoo", "last_name": "Shim", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": ""}], "custom_fields": [], "figshare_url": "https://acs.figshare.com/articles/journal_contribution/Utilization_of_Cold_Energy_of_LNG_for_Carbon_Dioxide_Capture_and_Liquefaction_in_Amine-Based_SMR/26107027", "download_disabled": false, "description": "Hydrogen\nis widely produced by a steam methane reforming (SMR)\nprocess, but CO<sub>2</sub> that is produced as a byproduct needs\nto be captured. For this purpose, amine absorption, one of the main\ncarbon capture and utilization (CCU) techniques, is commonly integrated\nwith the SMR process. A drawback of the amine absorption process is\nthat captured CO<sub>2</sub> is recovered in the gas phase and must\nbe liquefied or solidified to be suitable for transportation and storage.\nTraditionally, the liquefaction of CO<sub>2</sub> is achieved by a\nseries of compression-cooling and expansions (e.g., the Linde\u2013Hampson\nprocess), which is a costly and energy-intensive process. This study\nproposes using liquefied natural gas (LNG) not only as the source\nfor hydrogen production and heat supply for the SMR reaction, but\nalso to provide the necessary thermal energy requirement for the liquefaction\nof CO<sub>2</sub> using plate-fin heat exchangers. Aspen HYSYS is\nused to simulate a 300 Nm<sup>3</sup>/h scale SMR process, the amine\nabsorber, and CO<sub>2</sub> Liquefaction processes. Energy, exergy,\nand cost efficiencies for a conventional Linde\u2013Hampson process\nand two novel setups utilizing LNG cold energy are studied and compared.\nIn summary, our results show the significant advantages of the proposed\nnonrecycling process over the conventional Linde\u2013Hampson system.\nSpecifically, it offers up to a 39.38% enhancement in overall exergy\nefficiency, achieves a net rational exergy efficiency as high as 95.72%,\nand reduces total capital costs by 30.83%.", "funding": null, "funding_list": [], "version": 1, "status": "public", "size": 622769, "created_date": "2024-06-26T14:23:47Z", "modified_date": "2024-06-26T14:23:47Z", "is_public": true, "is_confidential": false, "is_metadata_record": false, "confidential_reason": "", "metadata_reason": "", "license": {"value": 10, "name": "CC BY-NC 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/"}, "tags": ["steam methane reforming", "main carbon capture", "carbon dioxide capture", "fin heat exchangers", "proposed nonrecycling process", "overall exergy efficiency", "2 </ sub", "based smr hydrogen", "amine absorpti